<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bertrand Russell</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1872">1872</a>–<a href="/years/1970">1970</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Bertrand Arthur William Russell, 3rd Earl Russell</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bertrand Russell, 3rd Earl Russell, was an English mathematician and philosopher and a founder of analytic philosophy, who did pioneering work in logic, set theory and the philosophy of language. A grandson of the prime minister John Russell, 1st Earl Russell, he studied mathematics at Trinity College, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambridge">Cambridge</a>, and with Alfred North Whitehead wrote <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/principia-mathematica-newton">Principia</a> Mathematica (1910–1913), a major attempt to reduce the whole of mathematics to logic. He went to prison for his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pacifism">pacifism</a> during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>, later condemned the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>' involvement in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam-war">Vietnam War</a> and campaigned for nuclear disarmament. He received the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nobel-prize">Nobel Prize</a> in Literature in 1950.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bertrand-russell.jpg" alt="Bertrand Russell" width="330" height="412" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bassano Ltd · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bertrand_Russell_in_1936.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Russell was born on 18 May 1872 at Ravenscroft, a country house in Trellech, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monmouthshire">Monmouthshire</a>, to John Russell, Viscount Amberley, and Katharine, Viscountess Amberley, both early advocates of birth control. His mother died of diphtheria in June 1874, followed shortly by his sister Rachel, and his father died in January 1876. He was raised by his paternal grandparents at Pembroke Lodge in Richmond Park, chiefly by his grandmother, the Countess Russell, whose progressive views on social justice and standing up for principle remained with him all his life.</p><p>He began at Trinity College, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambridge">Cambridge</a>, in 1890 on a scholarship to read for the Mathematical Tripos, graduating seventh Wrangler in 1893 and becoming a Fellow in philosophy in 1895. His first major publication was An Essay on the Foundations of Geometry (1897).</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Russell started in British idealism, the dominant philosophy of the time, but his friendship with G. E. Moore led him to analytic philosophy, and the two led a revolt against idealism. With Whitehead he wrote Principia Mathematica, published in 1910–1913. His article On Denoting has been considered a paradigm of philosophy. In 1916 he was convicted under the Defence of the Realm Act 1914 for his pacifist activity in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> and dismissed from Trinity. After a campaign by the Fellows, he was offered reinstatement and accepted it in 1920, but he resigned in 1921.</p><p>In August 1920 he visited Soviet Russia as part of a delegation and met <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vladimir-lenin">Vladimir Lenin</a>, whom he found disappointing. The visit ended his tentative support for the revolution, and he wrote The Practice and Theory of Bolshevism. He opposed rearmament against Nazi Germany, but changed his view in 1940 and in 1943 adopted a position he called relative political <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pacifism">pacifism</a>, that war may in extreme circumstances be the lesser of two evils. His History of Western Philosophy (1945) was a best-seller.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Russell is credited as one of the founders of analytic philosophy, and Willard Van Orman Quine named his work as a chief influence on his own. He made language a central part of philosophy and influenced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ludwig-wittgenstein">Ludwig Wittgenstein</a>, Gilbert Ryle, J. L. Austin and P. F. Strawson. In lectures of 1918 he set out logical atomism, the view that knowledge can be reduced to atomic propositions and their truth-functional compounds.</p><p>He argued that religion is largely harmful and was a member of the advisory council of the British Humanist Association. He became the 3rd Earl Russell in 1931 and held the title until his death on 2 February 1970.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bertrand Russell” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>John Dewey arrived in 1919 and lectured for a year, and <b>Bertrand Russell</b> lectured widely to warm crowds.</blockquote><a href="/movement/new-culture-movement">New Culture Movement · People</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Bertrand Russell</b> held that tariffs, sanitary regulation and a just system of distribution could hardly be managed without central government, and Robert Nozick argued in Anarchy, State, and Utopia that a minimal “night-watchman state” would arise from anarchy by an invisible hand.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/anarchism">Anarchism · Critiques &amp; Debates</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Bertrand Russell</b> argued that it weakens the individual’s capacity to judge his country’s foreign policy; Albert Einstein called it “an infantile disease … the measles of mankind”.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/nationalism">Nationalism · Critiques &amp; Debates</a></li><li><blockquote>Some pacifists changed their position: <b>Bertrand Russell</b> called his stance on Nazism relative pacifism, and E. M. Forster and Leonard Woolf rejected their earlier pacifism.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/pacifism">Pacifism · Critiques &amp; Debates</a></li><li><blockquote>…and Its Enemies of 1945, called Plato's state a totalitarian dystopia and held that it was meant as a topical political manifesto; <b>Bertrand Russell</b> argued that it was meant as a practical form of government, and G. A. Cohen that it was not totalitarian in the strict sense.</blockquote><a href="/work/the-republic">The Republic · Legacy</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Trellech, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. Died in Penrhyndeudraeth, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=51.75,-2.73&amp;year=1935" aria-label="Trellech on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4488 374 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Bertrand Russell"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4938 644h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4938 644h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=51.75,-2.73&amp;year=1935">What was here in 1935? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;The value of philosophy is, in fact, to be sought largely in its very uncertainty.&#8221;</p><footer>The Problems of Philosophy (1912), ch. 15 &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/5827/pg5827-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;…because these questions enlarge our conception of what is possible, enrich our intellectual imagination and diminish the dogmatic assurance which closes the mind against speculation…&#8221;</p><footer>The Problems of Philosophy (1912), ch. 15 &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/5827/pg5827-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;A life lived in this spirit—the spirit that aims at creating rather than possessing—has a certain fundamental happiness, of which it cannot be wholly robbed by adverse circumstances.&#8221;</p><footer>Proposed Roads to Freedom (1918), ch. 8 &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/690/pg690-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><details class="enc-q-more"><summary>2 more</summary><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;The glorification of the State, and the doctrine that it is every citizen's duty to serve the State, are radically against progress and against liberty.&#8221;</p><footer>Proposed Roads to Freedom (1918), ch. 5 &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/690/pg690-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;The man who has no tincture of philosophy goes through life imprisoned in the prejudices derived from common sense, from the habitual beliefs of his age or his nation…&#8221;</p><footer>The Problems of Philosophy (1912), ch. 15 &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/5827/pg5827-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote></details><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 595 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
